From: Codex <codex@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] server, win32u: let the external taskbar read other processes' window icons

Purpose: show the right icons for other processes' windows on the XP
taskbar, including the proxy windows of Linux applications.
Wine before: win32u refused WM_GETICON across processes and rejected
foreign icon handles, so the taskbar fell back to the default icon.
GetClassLongPtr returned no class icons for other processes' windows; the
XP taskbar then reads the application's file name from its PEB, which a
32-bit taskbar cannot do for a 64-bit process (WoW64).
Change: allow WM_GETICON across processes. The owner also gives its class
icons to the server when it creates a window or changes them, and
GetClassLongPtr(GCLP_HICON, GCLP_HICONSM) returns them to other processes,
as on Windows. The returned HICON stays the
owner's borrowed handle; the server keeps an immutable pixel snapshot of
static icons (up to 256x256, 2048 bytes of resource data each, 16 MiB per
owner and 64 MiB in all, freed when the owner destroys the icon or exits),
and GetIconInfo, DrawIconEx and CopyIcon in a reader build a private copy
from it (at most 64 cached, pinned while used and revalidated on every
read). Foreign icons cannot be changed or destroyed; animated and larger
icons are not shared. The first display DC is created outside the user
lock.
Evidence: tests/foreign-icons.c runs two processes with self-authored
pixels: identity, GetIconInfo, alpha and monochrome DrawIconEx, CopyIcon,
destroy refusal, cache pressure, handle reuse and owner exit, and class
icons (both, large only, changed by the owner); with --owner the owner is
the other architecture's build (all four pairs pass; the class icon checks
failed before).
Scope: only with WIN4WINE_EXTERNAL_SHELL; the server protocol changes, so
wineserver, ntdll and win32u must come from the same build.
Old patches: 0025.
